# console-gui-shim

`console-gui-shim` is a small WinOS-only helper executable for GUI applications that still need to behave well when launched from a command prompt.

On WinOS, a GUI executable does not stay attached to the console the way a normal console program does. That means standard input, output, and error handling can be awkward when the same application should work both from Explorer and from `cmd.exe`/PowerShell. This project provides the console-side shim for that scenario.

## Prerequisites

- **WinOS**: This project is WinOS-only and will not build on other platforms.
- **GNU Make**: Version 4.0 or later is required (`make --version` to check).
  - Available via [Scoop](https://scoop.sh/) (`scoop install make`), [MSYS2](https://www.msys2.org/), or standalone builds.
- **C++ Compiler** (one of the following):
  - `clang` / `clang++` (LLVM-based, default if available)
  - `gcc` / `g++` (MinGW-w64 recommended)
  - `cl` (Microsoft Visual C++ Build Tools)
  - `bcc32` / `embcc32` (Borland/Embarcadero C++, legacy support)

## What It Does

The program built from [`src/console-gui-shim.cpp`](src/console-gui-shim.cpp) is intended to be renamed with a `.com` extension and placed beside a same-named GUI `.exe`.

When invoked:

1. It finds its own executable path.
2. It rewrites the extension from `.com` to `.exe`.
3. It launches that GUI executable with the original command line.
4. It creates pipes for `stdin`, `stdout`, and `stderr`.
5. It forwards console I/O between the terminal and the GUI process.

This relies on a useful WinOS command-resolution behavior: when both `name.com` and `name.exe` exist, `cmd.exe` prefers the `.com` file when you type `name`.

## Expected Deployment Pattern

The intended final layout is:

```text
your-app.com   <- this shim
your-app.exe   <- the real GUI application
```

From a shell, users can type:

```powershell
your-app [args...]
```

and the shim will start `your-app.exe` while preserving console-style I/O behavior.

